.TH CHTTPSVR_REGISTER_HANDLER 3 "2026-08-03" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_register_handler \- register a buffered handler on the root router
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_register_handler(chttpsvr srv, chttp_method_t method,
                           const char *pattern,
                           chttpsvr_handler_fn fn, void *ctx);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Registers fn on the root router as the handler for the requests that
match method and pattern.  pattern is a list of segments separated by
forward slashes, and a segment of the form {name} is a named path
parameter.

With a method of CHTTP_ANY the route is a wildcard that matches any
of the seven real HTTP methods (CHTTP_GET, CHTTP_POST, CHTTP_PUT,
CHTTP_DELETE, CHTTP_PATCH, CHTTP_HEAD and CHTTP_OPTIONS) on the given
pattern, and the handler can call chttpsvr_req_method to find out
which method really arrived.  Registration order sets the priority:
a route for one method that you register before a CHTTP_ANY route on
the same pattern wins for its own method, and the CHTTP_ANY route
catches the other six.

A route for CHTTP_GET also serves HEAD requests for the same path, as
RFC 9110 section 9.3.2 requires.  The handler runs exactly as it does
for GET, and the server sends the headers of the response without the
body, content-length included whenever the handler wrote a body.  A
route registered explicitly for CHTTP_HEAD or for CHTTP_ANY on the
same path wins over this fallback, whichever of the two you register
first, so a path that must answer HEAD differently from GET can do
so.  A path that has a route for neither GET nor HEAD answers 405 to
a HEAD request.

CHTTP_ANY exists only for registration and is never the method of a
request that really arrives.  A request whose method the server does
not recognize at all (a WebDAV verb such as PROPFIND, a verb such as
TRACE or CONNECT, or a custom verb) never reaches a handler, one
registered with CHTTP_ANY included: the server rejects it with 501
Not Implemented before routing runs, and even before it parses the
path.  chttpsvr_req_method(3) describes the guarantee that a handler
gets from this.

pattern must start with '/', and a pattern that does not is rejected
at once with ccol_invalid_args.

The name inside a {name} segment may contain only characters from
[A-Za-z0-9_].  A name with any other character, such as a space, a
hyphen or a dot, is rejected with ccol_invalid_args, because
chttpsvr_req_param could never look it up.  A pattern that uses the
same {name} more than once, for example /a/{id}/b/{id}, is rejected
with ccol_invalid_args too, because the value that the second one
captures could never be reached: chttpsvr_req_param always returns
the FIRST name that matches.

The values of path segments, literal segments and captured parameters
alike, are decoded by the path rules of RFC 3986, so a percent-encoded
sequence such as %20 is decoded but a '+' stays a literal '+'.  That
matches the decoded path that chttpsvr_req_path returns.  A query
string is decoded differently, by the application/x-www-form-urlencoded
rules where a '+' reads as a space, but that applies only to
chttpsvr_req_query and chttpsvr_req_query_one and never to a path
parameter.

A mounted prefix owns every path under it, and the most specific mount
that owns a path handles the request alone; see chttpsvr_subrouter(3).
The routes that this function registers sit on the root router, which
handles only the paths under no mount.  So a root-level pattern never
answers a path under a mount and never skips the middleware of that
mount, however broad the pattern is and whenever you registered it;
registration order plays no part in this.  Inside one router the
server tries the routes in registration order.

The router looks at every registered route for a match on the path
and the method, so you can register several methods for the same
path and every one of them is reachable, whatever order you register
them in.  When at least one route matches the path but none matches
the method, the server answers 405 with an Allow header, as RFC 9110
section 15.5.6 requires.  The header lists every method that a route
of the handling router accepts for the path, in the order GET, HEAD,
POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH, OPTIONS, and HEAD is in it wherever a
CHTTP_GET route serves it.  When no route matches the path, the
answer is 404.  For a path under a mount, the owning sub-router gives
the 404 or the 405 and its middleware runs for it.  A request whose
method the server does not recognize at all is rejected earlier than
that, with 501, before any route matching runs; see the notes on
CHTTP_ANY above.

Path matching checks the percent-encoded sequences as well.  A request
with an invalid encoding in any path segment does not match the route
and gets a 404, whatever methods you registered for that pattern.
The bad segment can be a literal one, for example /bad%ZZusers/{id}
against /users/{id}, or a captured {name} parameter, for example
/users/bad%ZZvalue against /users/{id}.  A percent-encoded sequence
that decodes to a NUL byte (%00) counts as an invalid encoding too,
and is not decoded and compared as it is.

You can register the same pair of method and pattern more than once,
and each call succeeds with ccol_success, but the first one wins: the
server only ever calls the FIRST handler that you registered.

A pattern with two consecutive slashes, for example /foo//bar, is
rejected at registration time, and so is one with a trailing slash,
for example /foo/.  The server does not treat a trailing slash on a
request path as equivalent either, so a request for /users/42/ does
not match the pattern /users/{id}, and since a pattern must not end
in '/' you cannot register the trailing-slash form as a route of its
own.  If the server has to accept such a path, normalize it before
it reaches the server.

The handler runs on a thread of the worker pool of the server once the
whole body has arrived, and chttpsvr_req_body then returns that body.
No thread waits for a slow client in the meantime: a body that stops
arriving parks its connection without a thread, and any free worker
continues it later.  So the thread that runs the handler can be a
different one from the thread that began to read the body, and a
handler must not assume thread affinity across requests.  A response
that meets a full socket parks in the same way after the handler
returns.  See "Slow clients" in chttpsvr_start(3).

This function is thread-safe and you can call it at any time, also
while another thread runs chttpsvr_destroy on the same server; a call
that races such a destroy returns ccol_invalid_args and touches no
freed memory.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args when srv is CHTTPSVR_NULL or a stale handle that
                  was destroyed; when method is neither one of the
                  seven methods nor CHTTP_ANY; when pattern or fn is
                  NULL; when pattern does not start with '/', has two
                  consecutive slashes or has a trailing slash; when a
                  segment of the form {name} contains a character
                  outside [A-Za-z0-9_]; or when the same {name}
                  appears more than once.
ccol_not_enough_memory when an allocation fails.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler (3),
.BR chttpsvr_use (3),
.BR chttpsvr_subrouter (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_context (3)
